{"name":"Duck Green","hex":"#53665c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"duck","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To quickly lower (the head or body), often in order to prevent it from being struck by something.","To lower (something) into water; to thrust or plunge under liquid and suddenly withdraw."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cave passage containing water with low, or no, airspace."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/duck"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Duck","blocks":[{"text":"Duck is the common name for numerous species of waterfowl in the family Anatidae. Ducks are generally smaller and shorter-necked than swans and geese, which are members of the same family. Divided among several subfamilies, they are a form taxon; they do not represent a monophyletic group, since swans and geese are not considered ducks. Ducks are mostly aquatic birds, and may be found in both fresh water and sea water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Bucephala-albeola-010.jpg/330px-Bucephala-albeola-010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duck"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Pacific_Black_Ducks_on_pond_ducking.jpg/500px-Pacific_Black_Ducks_on_pond_ducking.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Pacific_Black_Ducks_on_pond_ducking.jpg/1280px-Pacific_Black_Ducks_on_pond_ducking.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Pacific_Black_Ducks_on_pond_ducking.jpg/500px-Pacific_Black_Ducks_on_pond_ducking.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pacific black duck displaying the characteristic upending \"duck\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APacific_Black_Ducks_on_pond_ducking.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Mallard-drake-chicago-march-2024.jpg/500px-Mallard-drake-chicago-march-2024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Mallard-drake-chicago-march-2024.jpg/1280px-Mallard-drake-chicago-march-2024.jpg?utm_sour